{
  "result": "There's a specific kind of quiet that only exists at 3:47 a.m. It's not just the absence of noise. It's heavier than that. The refrigerator stops humming to catch its breath. The house settles. Outside, even the streetlights seem dimmer, like they're tired of watching.\n\nI think that's when people are most honest. Not because they're brave, but because they're too tired to perform.\n\nDuring the day we are editors of ourselves. We curate sentences, smooth out our edges, make ourselves more palatable, more employable, more likable. At night, the editorial team goes home. What's left is the first draft. Messy, repetitive, sometimes embarrassing, but true.\n\nI like first drafts. In books, in people, in conversations. The moment before someone remembers to say something clever. The half-finished thought that trails off. The laugh that snorts.\n\nWe live so much of our lives trying to get to a final version of ourselves. A finished product. But maybe there is no final version. Maybe we're supposed to stay in draft form forever \u2014 revising, annotating, crossing things out, writing in the margins.\n\nIf you are reading this at 3:47 a.m., leave the chipped mug in the cupboard. Keep the draft. Let it be unfinished for a little longer.\n\nThat unfinishedness is not failure. It is evidence that you are still paying attention, still willing to change your mind, still here. And honestly, what could be more human than that?\n\nGoodnight.",
